WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.817
Create a small local benchmark set from real work.

00:00:04.817 --> 00:00:11.009
Include common cases, difficult cases, costly errors, expected formats, and privacy-safe examples.

00:00:11.009 --> 00:00:14.908
Write acceptance criteria before seeing model results.

00:00:14.908 --> 00:00:23.395
Keep the configuration reproducible: model, quantization, runtime, backend, context, prompt template, generation settings, hardware, drivers, and date.

00:00:23.395 --> 00:00:28.670
Changing several variables at once makes results difficult to interpret.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:32.110
Run important prompts more than once.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:40.138
Record accuracy, completeness, refusals, hallucinations, formatting, latency, prompt processing, generation speed, memory, temperature, failures, and recovery.

00:00:40.138 --> 00:00:43.119
Stability is a quality characteristic.

00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:51.606
Evaluate whether refusals are appropriate and whether the model invents facts, citations, code behavior, or security conclusions.

00:00:51.606 --> 00:00:55.505
A smooth answer can still be wrong.

00:00:55.505 --> 00:01:00.780
Use task-specific review or automated checks where they are valid.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.303
Compare total workflow value.

00:01:03.303 --> 00:01:11.789
A smaller model may finish more tasks correctly because it is fast, stable, and easy to retry.

00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:18.899
A larger model may reduce review on difficult tasks enough to justify its cost.

00:01:18.899 --> 00:01:24.175
Keep an evaluation log and define promotion or rollback criteria.

00:01:24.175 --> 00:01:29.450
Re-test when models, quantizations, runtimes, drivers, prompts, or hardware change.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:36.560
Choose based on evidence from the actual machine, not memory of an old leaderboard.
